<binaryimage arch="AARCH64:LE:64:v8A">
<!--
    A constant integer written into a byte array keeps its native width.
    P3 simplification (`option bytestorewidth on|off`, default off,
    p3_dataflow/kuna_bytestorewidth.rs in SplitDatatype).

    fill stores the 4-byte integer 3 at +0x18 of the node alloc_node returns
    (x86-64 `movl $0x3,0x18(%rax)`, AArch64 `str w1,[x0,#24]`, PowerPC32
    `stw r10,24(r9)`); fill_text stores "abc" with its NUL there (GCC's
    inlined `memcpy`). The script loads byte_store_width_{x86_64,aarch64,ppc32}.so
    (source byte_store_width.c, recipe in the fixtures README) and states
    alloc_node's return (on PowerPC32 its PIC call stub at 0x310) as a View
    whose 20-byte `char` array starts the second element at +0x18, or as a
    record of four named byte fields there.

    No upstream issue: reported for a record view in an x86-64 shared library.
    Assert #1 is the default (option off) pass: upstream SplitDatatype splits
    the one word store into four byte assignments. Assert #2 is the option-on
    pass: the store prints at its own width. Assert #3: with the option on, the
    text constant still splits into its characters. Assert #4: with the option
    on, a write into four named byte fields still splits into those fields.
-->
  <bytechunk space="ram" offset="0x1000" readonly="true">c0035fd6</bytechunk>
  <symbol space="ram" offset="0x1000" name="harness_stub"/>
</binaryimage>
